BUG REPORT FROM USER: 'I wanted to share a custom argument class between several commands, so I built the decorator once and applied it to more than one command:

    my_arg = click.argument("name", cls=MyArgument)

    @click.command()
    @my_arg
    def first(name): ...

    @click.command()
    @my_arg
    def second(name): ...

I expected both commands to use my custom argument class, but only the first one does - the second command ends up with a plain click.Argument instead. The same thing happens with click.option(..., cls=...) when the decorator is reused, and it is confusing because nothing warns me that the decorator can only be applied once.'

Your job: find the root cause (look at src/click/decorators.py: _param_memo / option / argument decorators, and how Parameter objects are attached to Command.params - a single Parameter instance is shared and mutated, e.g. its `name`/`opts` are rewritten by the second command, or the same object is reused), decide the correct minimal fix so that reusing the same decorator object on several commands gives each command its own correctly-configured parameter, fix it in the source, and verify.

IMPORTANT CONSTRAINTS: (a) Do NOT modify or delete any pre-existing test in /click/tests; pre-existing green tests must keep passing. (b) Only fix this bug; no unrelated changes, no new features. (c) No internet. (d) First record the baseline: `cd /click && python -m pytest tests -q` (note: other agents' in-flight edits may make the tree noisy; also extract a pristine baseline with `git -C /click archive HEAD | tar -x -C /tmp/pristineA` and run pytest there if needed). Then implement the fix and run the full suite again to confirm no pre-existing test broke. Write throwaway verification scripts under /tmp (NOT in the repo) reproducing the user's exact snippet (both commands, and the option variant) and confirm both commands get the custom class. Report back: root cause, exact file+lines changed, the diff of your change only, test results (baseline vs after), and any pre-existing failures observed.

